Operating under a modest, single-machine setup—an off-the-shelf laptop CPU (Apple M4 Pro) with zero GPU acceleration and a total API expenditure under \$4.00—we evaluate:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Direct LLM Reasoning:} Frontier LLMs (the Claude 5.5 series: Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, and Haiku 5.5) operating in adaptive thinking mode with a 32,000-token output ceiling to solve puzzles directly in-context.

Our findings reveal striking disparities across these paradigms:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Direct LLM reasoning is expensive, slow, and fragile.} When reasoning internal to the context window, frontier models solve only 30\%--60\% of attempted instances, consuming 105 to 166 seconds of wall-clock time and up to \$0.71 per solved puzzle.

\textbf{Failure Logging and Metrics.} Direct solving runs are monitored for three distinct termination criteria:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textit{Solved:} The model outputs a complete 81-digit assignment that matches all given clues and satisfies every row, column, and $3 \times 3$ box constraint.

The scalar energy $E(\mathbf{X})$ is defined as:
